# A short story for the Aside engine.
# Warm, wry, and deliberately in a register my other work does not use: the others are about memory,
# performance and being watched. This one is about tending something, and about the difference between
# doing a job correctly and doing it well. Six scenes, three endings, and it fits in one sitting.

title: The Lamp Room
author: Roxanne
start: arrive

== arrive ==
bg cliff_path
music fan_hum
The cliff path ends at a door that was painted white some decades ago.
Sea on three sides. Wind that gets into your collar like it has been waiting for you.

You are the new keeper. The letter said the hours were long and the company was quiet,
which is the sort of thing a letter says when it means both of them.

* Knock. It is somebody's house. -> knock
* Try the handle. You are expected. -> enter

== knock ==
sfx door_open
You knock. Nothing happens for long enough that you start composing an apology.

Then the door opens on a woman in a coat that has clearly been slept in,
holding a mug in both hands like it is the only warm thing left in the county.

keeper: You knocked.
keeper: Thirty years, and nobody knocks.
keeper: Come in before you freeze into an anecdote.
~ knocked true
-> meet

== enter ==
sfx door_open
The door is open. It has been open all night.

Inside there is a room, a chair, and a hundred and eleven steps of iron staircase going up
to a light that is doing most of the work of being seen from the sea.

keeper: You did not knock.
keeper: Good. Nothing in this tower needs permission.
~ knocked false
-> meet

== meet ==
bg lamp_room
show keeper neutral at left
keeper: The lamp goes out at some point tonight. It always does, near the small hours.
keeper: When it does, you will want to do something clever. Everybody does.

She sits down, which is her way of saying the conversation is over,
and also her way of saying she intends to stay in the room for it.

* Ask her about the lamp. -> lamp
* Ask her why she stayed. -> why

== why ==
keeper: Because the alternative was a town.
keeper: A town does not need you at three in the morning.
keeper: This does.
~ knows_why true
-> lamp

== lamp ==
bg lamp_room
music music_box
The light stutters, browns, and goes.
The sea outside does not react, because the sea has seen this before and is not impressed.

The lamp room smells of hot dust and old oil. The wick needs trimming, the glass needs wiping,
and somewhere below there is a can of oil you have not seen since you set your bag down.

* Trim the wick by the book. -> by_the_book
* Put oil in first, then the wick. -> oil_first
* Sit down on the floor beside her and wait. -> sit

== by_the_book ==
sfx clank
You trim the wick, wipe the glass, and light it the way the manual describes,
because the manual is thirty pages long and somebody wrote all of them.
The light comes back. It is correct. It is exactly as bright as it should be.
keeper: Good.
keeper: Same as the book, then. Somebody wrote those pages for a reason.
-> dawn_book

== oil_first ==
sfx clank
You find the can under your own bag, which is where you left it, which is where it would
have been found anyway. Sometimes competence is just remembering where you put things.
You oil it, then you trim it, then you light it.
keeper: Slower than the book.
keeper: Also warmer. Whoever writes the books is not up here at three.
~ did_well true
-> dawn_together

== sit ==
You sit on the floor. The stone is cold and she does not say anything
and after a while neither do you, and the light comes back on its own,
which is what she knew and what she was waiting to see you find out.
keeper: There. It only needed somebody in the room.
~ sat true
-> dawn_together

== dawn_book ==
bg cliff_dawn
The morning comes the way mornings do: without ceremony and slightly early.
She shows you where the oil lives and does not mention the book.

* Ask her whether it gets easier. -> dawn_book_answer [if knows_why]
* Say goodnight. -> END

== dawn_book_answer ==
keeper: No.
keeper: You just get better at the hours.
-> END

== dawn_together ==
bg cliff_dawn
The morning comes the way mornings do: without ceremony and slightly early.
She does not say anything for a while, and then she says the thing you will end up
telling whoever comes after you.

keeper: The light is not the job.
keeper: The light is what happens when you do the job.

* Tell her you will be back tomorrow. -> dawn_promise [if did_well]
* Tell her you will be back, and mean it. -> dawn_promise [if sat]
* Ask her what she will do now. -> dawn_ask [if knocked]
* Say nothing, and watch the light. -> END

== dawn_promise ==
keeper: I know.
keeper: You have the hands for it. That is not a compliment, it is a forecast.
-> END

== dawn_ask ==
keeper: Sleep, probably. For a week.
keeper: Then I will come back up and check you have not painted the door a different colour.
-> END
